#### Understanding filter patterns (the glob)

```json
"files": [
    "tsmaps/**/*.tsstp",
    "tsmaps/**/*.tsvar"
],
```

The text inside each entry e.g. `"tsmaps/**/*.tsvar"` in the array is called a **glob pattern**. 
It works like a wildcard:
- `*` means “anything here.”
- `**` means “any folder depth.”
- So `"tsmaps/**/*.tsstp"` means: *“Look inside the `tsmaps` folder and every subfolder, for any file ending in `.tsstp`.”*

> **Note:** The paths in the `files` array are relative to the location of the `.cspell.json` file. If `.cspell.json` is in the project root, `"tsmaps/**"` points to the `tsmaps` folder next to it.

#### Add a new pattern

If your file is in a different folder, add a new line to the list. For example, to also analyse Markdown files in a `notes` folder alongside the `.cspell.json` file, you would edit the array to look like this:

```json
"files": [
    "tsmaps/**/*.tsstp",
    "tsmaps/**/*.tsvar",
    "notes/**/*.md"
],
```

{{::vs.Vscode_JsonArray_CommaGoldenRule}}


#### Edit an existing pattern

If a pattern is almost right but not quite, you can change it. For example, if the pattern is `"tsmaps/*.tsstp"` (which only checks files directly in `tsmaps`, not subfolders), you can change it to `"tsmaps/**/*.tsstp"` to include subfolders.

